A Mean-Field Framework for Inference-Time Distributional Control of Diffusion Models Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2608.08770v1 [stat.ML] 09 Aug 2026 A Mean-Field Framework for Inference-Time Distributional Control of Diffusion Models Samuel Howard Affiliation: Department of Statistics, University of Oxford Correspondence to: howard@stats.ox.ac.uk Nikolas Nüsken Affiliation: Department of Mathematics, King’s College London Abstract Diffusion models are increasingly used as controllable samplers, whose generations can be steered at inference time according to a chosen reward function. While such rewards are typically defined on individual samples, for many applications it is desirable to steer according to distribution-level rewards, for example to calibrate with population-level information or to encourage diversity. In both cases, simply incorporating the reward gradient into the dynamics, while often effective, comes with few theoretical guarantees on the sampled distribution. For pointwise rewards, recent work has therefore sought to develop a principled framework for targeting a prescribed tilted distribution using particle reweighting. However, an analogous theoretically-grounded approach for distributional rewards is currently lacking. In this work, we formulate inference-time distributional control as targeting a tilted measure under a mean-field framework, and derive a weighted interacting particle scheme to target it in a principled manner. Our framework recovers pointwise-reward steering as a special case, while providing a theoretical foundation for existing batch-level steering methods. Empirically, we verify that the procedure correctly targets the prescribed distribution in tractable low-dimensional settings, and investigate its behaviour in higher-dimensional protein conformation tasks. 1 Introduction Figure 1 : (Left) Standard Inference-Time Steering: For pointwise rewards, the reward-gradients ∇ r t 
abla r_{t} can be incorporated into the inference dynamics, to steer the particles independently towards areas of higher reward. Feynman-Kac corrections are applied to the log-weights A t A_{t} to ensure the weighted particles trace the correct marginals μ t ∗ ​ ( ⋅ ) ∝ p t ​ ( ⋅ ) ​ e r t ​ ( ⋅ ) \mu_{t}^{*}(\cdot)\propto p_{t}(\cdot)e^{r_{t}(\cdot)} . (Right) Mean-Field Inference-Time Steering: For a measure-defined reward function ℛ ⁡ ( μ ) \mathcal{R}(\mu) , we incorporate the gradient of the first-variation, ∇ Ψ t ​ ( ⋅ , μ t ) 
abla\Psi_{t}(\cdot,\mu_{t}) as the steering term, leading to interaction between the particles. Through a mean-field framework, we derive the corrective reweighting procedure to ensure the reweighted interacting particles trace out the target tilted measures μ t ∗ ​ ( ⋅ ) ∝ p t ​ ( ⋅ ) ​ e Ψ t ​ ( ⋅ , μ ∗ ) \mu_{t}^{*}(\cdot)\propto p_{t}(\cdot)e^{\Psi_{t}(\cdot,\mu^{*})} . Diffusion and Flow Models Diffusion and flow-based generative models ( Song et al., 2021 ; Lipman et al., 2023 ; Liu et al., 2023 ; Albergo and Vanden-Eijnden, 2023 ) generate novel samples from a data distribution by learning time-dependent dynamics that transport an initial Gaussian distribution p 0 p_{0} to the target p 1 p_{1} . Such models have achieved state-of-the-art performance across a wide range of domains, including images ( Rombach et al., 2022 ) , molecules ( Watson et al., 2023 ) , and videos ( Ho et al., 2022 ) . Inference-Time Steering In many applications, the goal is not only to sample from the base distribution p 1 p_{1} , but to instead obtain samples that exhibit certain desired properties. To this end, one can ‘steer’ the generative process by including additional drift terms in the inference dynamics. Such steering mechanisms can be used to direct a pretrained generative model towards higher-quality samples, or samples that fulfil certain task-specific properties, without requiring further training. Most existing inference-time steering methods aim to improve samples with respect to a pointwise reward function r : ℝ d → ℝ r:{\mathbb{R}}^{d}ightarrow{\mathbb{R}} defined on individual generated samples. Early steering approaches generally incorporated the reward gradient ∇ r ​ ( X t ) 
abla r(X_{t}) into the inference dynamics, to ‘push’ the generations towards areas of higher reward ( Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021 ; Chung et al., 2023 ; Bansal et al., 2024 ) . While effective at improving sample quality, these heuristic modifications to the dynamics come with a poor theoretical understanding of the sampled distribution. This limitation has motivated a large number of recent works aiming to place inference-time steering on a firmer theoretical footing. To do so, the dominant paradigm aims to sample from the KL {\mathrm{KL}} -regularised objective, arg ​ max μ { 𝔼 X ∼ μ [ r ( X ) ] − KL ( μ ∥ p 1 ) } \argmax_{\mu} ig\{{\mathbb{E}}_{X\sim\mu}[r(X)]-{\mathrm{KL}}(\mu\|p_{1}) ig\} (1) which corresponds to the exponentially-tilted distribution μ 1 ∗ ​ ( x 1 ) ∝ p 1 ​ ( x 1 ) ​ e r ⁡ ( x 1 ) \mu^{*}_{1}(x_{1})\propto p_{1}(x_{1})e^{r(x_{1})} . Since gradient-steering alone does not sample from this target tilted measure, recent works have sought to correct for the discrepancy using Feynman-Kac particle reweighting schemes, enabling correct tracking of the desired distributional path via SMC resampling procedures ( Wu et al., 2023 ; Thornton et al., 2025 ; Singhal et al., 2025 ; Skreta et al., 2025 ; He et al., 2026 ; Sabour et al., 2025 ) . Distributional-Reward Steering Following these recent advances, steering according to pointwise-defined reward functions is now well-understood and based upon a principled theoretical foundation. However, many important control objectives are inherently distributional rather than pointwise. In many applications, rather than aiming for individual samples to score highly, one may wish to shape the overall generated distribution according to global properties, such as calibration to experimental observations, balancing across modes, or encouraging diversity. To formalise such distributional-steering, we will instead consider optimising according to a measure-defined reward function ℛ : 𝒫 ⁡ ( ℝ d ) → ℝ \mathcal{R}:\mathcal{P}({\mathbb{R}}^{d})ightarrow{\mathbb{R}} , arg ​ max μ { ℛ ( μ ) − KL ( μ ∥ p 1 ) } . \argmax_{\mu} ig\{\mathcal{R}(\mu)-{\mathrm{KL}}(\mu\|p_{1}) ig\}. (2) A number of existing works have incorporated steering terms that operate at the distributional level. For example, Corso et al. (2024) ; Kirchhof et al. (2025) ; Lam et al. (2026) steer towards generating diverse samples, Maddipatla et al. (2025) steer according to experimental observations, Saravanan et al. (2026) steer to increase expected information gain, and Sani et al. (2026) guide with MMD gradients. However, as in the pointwise-reward case, such approaches are largely heuristic in nature and it is not clear precisely what distribution these procedures target. Unlike in the pointwise-reward setting, a theoretically-grounded framework and approach for distributional inference-time steering of diffusion models is currently lacking. Contributions In this work, we address this gap by reconciling these two currently distinct literatures through a mean-field framework , allowing us to place distributional steering on a similarly principled footing to the pointwise case. In particular, we make the following contributions. • We formalise distribution-level steering ( 2 ) as aiming to sample from a tilted distribution for a measure-defined reward function ℛ \mathcal{R} , in which the tilt now depends on the measure itself. This gives a distributional analogue of the reward-tilted targets used in the pointwise case. • Using this mean-field framework, we derive weighted McKean-Vlasov dynamics whose marginals correctly track the desired tilted path. From this, we develop a novel weighted interacting-particle sampling procedure that enables principled distribution-level steering. • We show that our framework recovers standard pointwise reward steering as a special case, and provides a principled counterpart to existing gradient-steering methods for distributional control. Empirically, we validate that our procedure correctly targets the tilted distribution in verifiable low-dimensional settings, and examine its behaviour on higher-dimensional protein conformation tasks relative to standard gradient-steering approaches. 2 Background: Inference-Time Steering for Pointwise Rewards In this work, we will largely follow the presentation of Sabour et al. (2025) . To begin, let us consider a flow-based model given by the dynamics d ​ X t = [ b t ​ ( X t ) + ε t ​ s t ​ ( X t ) ] ​ d ​ t + 2 ​ ε t ​ d ​ B t . {\mathrm{d}}X_{t}= ig[b_{t}(X_{t})+\varepsilon_{t}s_{t}(X_{t}) ig]{\mathrm{d}}t+\sqrt{2\varepsilon_{t}}\,{\mathrm{d}}B_{t}. (3) Here, ε t \varepsilon_{t} is an arbitrary noise schedule, and s t s_{t} is the score function which can be obtained from a transformation of the vector field b t b_{t} (see Albergo et al. (2023) , for example). These dynamics trace out a path of marginals p t p_{t} which interpolates from p 0 p_{0} to the data distribution p 1 p_{1} . Given a reward r r defined on the generated samples x 1 x_{1} , inference-time steering methods aim to modify the generation procedure to instead sample from the reward-tilted distribution, which is the maximiser of the KL-regularised objective ( 1 ), μ ∗ ​ ( x ) ∝ p 1 ​ ( x ) ​ e r ⁡ ( x ) . \mu^{*}(x)\propto p_{1}(x)e^{r(x)}. (4) We remark that inference-time steering differs from the fine-tuning paradigm ( Clark et al., 2024 ; Xu et al., 2023 ; Domingo-Enrich et al., 2025 ) , as there is no additional training. Here, the dynamics are modified for the currently-chosen reward function r r , and different choices of r r can be used without having to perform extra training. Gradient-Steered Dynamics The aim of inference-time steering methods ( Singhal et al., 2025 ; Skreta et al., 2025 ; He et al., 2026 ; Sabour et al., 2025 ) is to ‘push’ the particles towards areas of higher reward during the generation, while performing SMC-style reweighting of the particles to ensure they trace out a prescribed path of marginal distributio